    file: "02-breathing-and-cardiac.md",
    markdown: "# Breathing and Cardiac Emergencies\nsource: Adapted from public-domain resuscitation council guidance\n\n## Adult CPR\nCheck for danger, then check for a response by shouting and squeezing the shoulders. If there is no response, shout for help and open the airway by tilting the head back and lifting the chin.\nLook, listen, and feel for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds. Occasional gasping is not normal breathing and is a sign of cardiac arrest.\nIf breathing is absent or abnormal, begin chest compressions. Place the heel of one hand in the centre of the chest, the other hand on top, and interlock the fingers.\nCompress hard and fast: 5 to 6 cm deep, at 100 to 120 compressions per minute. Allow the chest to recoil fully between compressions.\nGive 30 compressions followed by 2 rescue breaths. If you are unwilling or unable to give breaths, continuous compressions alone are far better than nothing.\nSwap rescuers every two minutes if possible, because compression quality falls sharply with fatigue.\nContinue until the person shows signs of life, a defibrillator is ready to use, or you are physically unable to continue.\n\n## Child and infant CPR\nFor a child, use one or two hands and compress to about one third of the chest depth. For an infant, use two fingers in the centre of the chest just below the nipple line.\nGive 5 initial rescue breaths before starting compressions, because cardiac arrest in children is usually caused by a breathing problem rather than a heart problem.\nUse a compression to breath ratio of 30 to 2 if you are alone, or 15 to 2 with two trained rescuers.\nDo not tilt an infant's head as far back as an adult's. Over-extending the neck closes a small airway rather than opening it.\n\n## Choking\nIf the person can cough forcefully, encourage them to keep coughing. Do not intervene while an effective cough continues.\nIf the cough becomes silent or weak, or they cannot breathe or speak, give 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand, leaning them forward.\nIf back blows fail, give 5 abdominal thrusts: stand behind, place a fist above the navel, grasp it with the other hand, and pull sharply inward and upward.\nAlternate 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until the obstruction clears or the person becomes unresponsive.\nIf they become unresponsive, lower them to the ground and begin CPR, checking the mouth for the dislodged object before each set of breaths.\nFor an infant, use 5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts. Never use abdominal thrusts on an infant.\nAnyone who has received abdominal thrusts should be assessed medically for internal injury.\n\n## Suspected heart attack\nTypical symptoms are central crushing chest pain or pressure lasting more than a few minutes, often spreading to the jaw, neck, or left arm, with sweating, nausea, and shortness of breath.\nWomen, older adults, and people with diabetes more often present without classic chest pain — instead with sudden fatigue, breathlessness, or indigestion-like discomfort.\nSit the person down, leaning back with knees bent. This position reduces the workload on the heart.\nIf they are not allergic and not already bleeding, give one 300 mg aspirin and ask them to chew it slowly. Chewing gets it into the bloodstream faster than swallowing.\nDo not let them walk to transport. Exertion during a heart attack can trigger cardiac arrest.\n\n## Stroke — the FAST check\nFace: ask them to smile, and look for one side drooping.\nArms: ask them to raise both arms, and see whether one drifts down.\nSpeech: ask them to repeat a simple sentence, and listen for slurring or the wrong words.\nTime: if any of these are abnormal, note the time symptoms began and seek emergency care immediately.\nThe time of onset determines which treatments are possible, so record it precisely. If they woke with symptoms, the relevant time is when they were last seen well.\nGive nothing to eat or drink. A stroke frequently impairs swallowing, and fluid entering the lungs causes pneumonia.\n",
